  },
  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (4x^2 + z^2)/2, drawn at 0.4 × loss and steeper along x. Each update: new = old − learning rate × (true slope + noise). The x direction overshoots when the learning rate is above 0.5. Each host step is one update.",
    "Noise is Gaussian and centred on the true slope. Its spread is 2·sqrt((64−b)/(63b)) for batch size b, which shrinks like sampling b of 64 points and is exactly 0 at b = 64. The noise is set by the run number and step, so Reset replays the same noise.",
    "Arrows are drawn at their true length in bowl units (scale 1). The orange arrow is the next mini-batch step the ball will take. The grey arrow is learning rate × the true downhill direction.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss goes above 8, which is the loss at the bowl's edge along x (diverged). Points that leave the bowl are drawn at its rim. The loss readout always shows the true value.",
    "Status reads 'ready' before the first step of a run. Setting changes take effect at the next step."
  ]
}